/// Chapter 31 of the guide, written from md-docs/31-performance.md by
/// scripts/site-from-guide.py; edit the guide, not this file.
page PerformanceGuide(path: "/docs/guide/performance", title: "Performance", description: "What a build does to be fast, wf build --stats, size budgets, paint timings from wf verify, and the few things that make a site slow.", layout: DocsShell("Performance", chapter: t("guide.chapter", { n: "31" }), blurb: "What a build already does to be fast, how to see what it weighs, budgets that warn, and the few things that make a site slow.", source: "md-docs/31-performance.md", sections: [Anchor(id: "what-the-build-does-already", label: "What the build does already"), Anchor(id: "seeing-what-it-weighs", label: "Seeing what it weighs"), Anchor(id: "budgets", label: "Budgets"), Anchor(id: "timing-it-in-a-browser", label: "Timing it in a browser"), Anchor(id: "what-makes-a-site-slow", label: "What makes a site slow")], prevTo: "/docs/guide/environments", prevLabel: "30. {t("ch.30")}", nextTo: "/docs/guide/interop", nextLabel: "32. {t("ch.32")}")) {
    Stack(gap: .lg) {
        Heading("What the build does already", id: "what-the-build-does-already").h2

        Markdown("- **Only the runtime you use.** The runtime is a set of modules; a build keeps the ones its code reaches. A page of static text carries 3 of its 37 modules: an `app.js` of 11 kB, 3.7 kB gzipped.\n- **A chunk per page.** `build.split` (on by default) writes each page's code and styles as their own files, loaded when its route is first shown.\n- **Pre-rendered HTML** with `build.ssg`: the first paint needs no script.\n- **One pruned stylesheet.** Only the built-ins you use, only the tokens something names.\n- **Precompressed.** A `.gz` beside every text file, for a host that serves it; `wf serve` sends the same bytes, so Lighthouse against the dev server measures what a deploy sends.\n- **Images at every width**, with `width`/`height` so nothing shifts, a placeholder while they load, the first one eager and the rest lazy ([Media](/docs/guide/media)).\n- **Minified** output (`build.minify`).")

        Heading("Seeing what it weighs", id: "seeing-what-it-weighs").h2

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "wf build --stats")
            ])

        CodeBlock(#"  What it weighs
    app.js                               47.6 kB  (14.6 kB gzipped)  +20 B
    styles.css                           16.9 kB  (3.7 kB gzipped)
    index.html                            5.1 kB  (1.3 kB gzipped)
    pages/Suggest.js                      2.2 kB  (0.8 kB gzipped)
    pages/Home.js                         1.4 kB  (0.6 kB gzipped)
    …
    total                                90.7 kB

  Runtime: 14 of 37 modules, 89.3 kB of 220.3 kB (before minifying)
    core          19.6 kB  always
    motion        16.6 kB  needed by when, match, each, router
    form          12.1 kB  WF.form
    router        10.7 kB  WF.router
    store          6.5 kB  WF.store
    …
    left out: helpers format offline peer scalars text slot show browser …

  Tokens: 26 nothing in the output names, left out"#, title: "text")

        Markdown("That is the [tutorial](/docs/guide/tutorial)'s site: a form brings `form` and `field`, a store `store` and `keep`, a second page `router`.\n\nEach build records its sizes in `.wf-sizes.json`, so the next `--stats` shows what moved. Each runtime module is listed with what reached it — the first place to look when a module you did not expect is in.")

        Heading("Budgets", id: "budgets").h2

        CodeBlock(#"{ "build": { "budget": { "app.js": "40 kB", "styles.css": "30 kB", "pages/Home.js": "10 kB" } } }"#, title: "json")

        Markdown("A gzipped size an output should stay under. Going over prints a warning and the build goes on; a CI job that wants to fail on it can read the same numbers.")

        Heading("Timing it in a browser", id: "timing-it-in-a-browser").h2

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "wf build && wf verify --budget 800")
            ])

        Markdown("`wf verify` loads every route in headless Chrome and prints its first contentful paint, the nodes in the document and the bytes and requests it took; `--budget` fails a route slower than that. Lighthouse and the browser's performance panel work on the build as on any site.")

        Heading("What makes a site slow", id: "what-makes-a-site-slow").h2

        Markdown("- **A large `data` file.** `data posts = \"posts.json\"` is inlined into the bundle, so a 2 MB file is 2 MB of JavaScript. Keep build-time data to what pages show; fetch the rest, or split it across pages that each read one.\n- **A waterfall of fetches.** A resource whose URL depends on another's answer waits for it. Where you can, ask for both at once, or have the server send them together.\n- **Layout decided by `viewport`.** It is unknown until the script runs, so the page paints one branch and swaps. Use responsive values, which are CSS.\n- **`runtime: \"full\"`.** It ships every module. Only needed when a hand-written script calls `WF` in ways the build cannot see.\n- **Web fonts.** Each weight is a download. Use `display=swap`, few weights, or the baseline's system fonts.\n- **Unkeyed lists that change.** `for item in items` rebuilds the list; `for item in items by item.id` moves the nodes that stayed.\n- **Pictures from `public/`.** They are copied as they are. Declare them with `image` to get every width and a placeholder.")
    }
}
